I said: Alright, let's take stock of where we are. We've got vehicles and weapons in — so the core combat loop is starting to breathe. Let me think about what's missing for an actual *game* here.

Right now I think the biggest gap is **the AI** — we need deer and hunters that actually move, fight, and make the battlefield feel alive. Without that, we've got a pretty tech demo but no war. Here's what I'm thinking for `ai.js`:

```js
// ai.js — FORESTWAR entity AI: deer herding behavior + hunter combat logic
import * as THREE from 'three';

export class EntityAI {
  constructor(mesh, type) {
    this.mesh = mesh;
    this.type = type;        // 'deer' | 'hunter'
    this.state = 'idle';     // idle, patrol, engage, flee, stampede
    this.target = null;
    this.health = type === 'deer' ? 60 : 100;
    this.speed = type === 'deer' ? 8 : 5;
    this.attackCooldown = 0;
    this.senseRadius = type === 'deer' ? 40 : 60;
  }

  update(dt, world, enemies) {
    this.attackCooldown -= dt;
    const nearest = this.findNearest(enemies);

    if (this.type === 'deer') this.updateDeer(dt, world, nearest);
    else this.updateHunter(dt, world, nearest);
  }

  findNearest(enemies) {
    let closest = null;
    let minDist = Infinity;
    for (const e of enemies) {
      const d = this.mesh.position.distanceTo(e.mesh.position);
      if (d < minDist) { minDist = d; closest = e; }
    }
    return minDist < this.senseRadius ? closest : null;
  }

  // Deer: flee individually, but group up when scared enough
  updateDeer(dt, world, threat) {
    if (threat && threat.mesh.position.distanceTo(this.mesh.position) < 25) {
      this.state = 'flee';
      const flee = this.mesh.position.clone()
        .sub(threat.mesh.position).normalize().multiplyScalar(this.speed * dt);
      this.mesh.position.add(flee);
    } else if (threat) {
      this.state = 'stampede'; // band together and CHARGE
      const charge = threat.mesh.position.clone()
        .sub(this.mesh.position).normalize().multiplyScalar(this.speed * 1.2 * dt);
      this.mesh.position.add(charge);
      if (this.mesh.position.distanceTo(threat.mesh.position) < 3 && this.attackCooldown <= 0) {
        threat.health -= 15; // ANTLER STRIKE
        this.attackCooldown = 1.5;
      }
    } else {
      this.state = 'patrol';
      this.mesh.position.x += (Math.random() - 0.5) * this.speed * dt;
      this.mesh.position.z += (Math.random() - 0.5) * this.speed * dt;
    }
    this.mesh.lookAt(threat ? threat.mesh.position : this.mesh.position.clone().add(flee));
  }

  // Hunters: keep distance, shoot
  updateHunter(dt, world, threat) {
    if (!threat) { this.state = 'patrol'; return; }
    const dist = this.mesh.position.distanceTo(threat.mesh.position);
    this.state = dist < 50 ? 'engage' : 'patrol';

    if (this.state === 'engage') {
      this.mesh.lookAt(threat.mesh.position);
      if (dist > 30) { // close in
        const dir = threat.mesh.position.clone().sub(this.mesh.position)
          .normalize().multiplyScalar(this.speed * dt);
        this.mesh.position.add(dir);
      } else if (dist < 15) { // back off
        const dir = this.mesh.position.clone().sub(threat.mesh.position)
          .normalize().multiplyScalar(this.speed * dt);
        this.mesh.position.add(dir);
      }
      if (this.attackCooldown <= 0 && dist < 50) {
        // hitscan shot — trace a ray, roll for hit
        if (Math.random() < 0.4) { threat.health -= 12; }
        this.attackCooldown = 0.8;
      }
    }
  }
}
```

The fun part here is the deer AI — I don't want them to just be targets. When they sense hunters but aren't cornered, they *stampede*. A herd of deer charging at you with antlers down is genuinely terrifying, and it flips the power dynamic in a way that makes this more interesting than a standard shooter.

What do you think — should I wire this up to a spawn manager next so we can actually drop 20 deer and 10 hunters into a forest and watch the chaos? Or do you want to tweak the AI behavior first?

